  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonably dry and temperature airmass (perhaps\na couple degrees below normal) is being ushered in behind this \nmorning's cold front as it pushes further offshore. In the near \nterm, low stratus will continue dissipating, leading to clear\nskies this evening. The high will become the dominant weather \nfeature for the weekend and much of next week. Cooler temperatures\nwith highs in the 60s prevail today and tomorrow before a \nslight warm-up next week into the low 70s, although humidity \nwill remain low. We will have to watch for the risk of some \nfrost over the upcoming nights, although chances have decreased\nfor tonight and Sunday night, and increased for Monday night.\nTonight a mixture of high clouds and light winds should keep low\ntemps around 40 inland. NBM had low temps in the mid to upper\n30s, but this seems to be an erroneous bias correction as all\nother available guidance is not that low, and high clouds/light\nwinds should prevent proper radiational cooling. Tomorrow night\nskies are clear, but light winds are still expected to continue,\nonce again lessening radiational cooling. Monday night however,\nwe have the high firmly overhead with clear skies and calm\nwinds, paired with dewpoints in the 30s. This results in the\nbest chance for frost concerns, and will have to be monitored in\nsubsequent updates. Manually brought the temps down by blending\nin NBM/NBM10 for the typical radiational cooling areas in\nCarteret, Onslow, Jones, and Duplin counties, with minTs in the\nmid 30s Tuesday morning in those locations. \n\nBeyond mid-week most guidance depicts an increasingly unsettled\npattern as complex mid-level low deepens over the eastern CONUS.\nThere is at least some signal for a developing low off our coast\nbeyond Wednesday, although its evolution and timing remain\nhighly uncertain.\n\nGuidance is pretty locked in on unseasonable cold temperatures\nnext weekend behind the low pressure system. While there is\ninherent uncertainty 7 days out, CPC does have a slight risk of\nmuch below normal temperatures for our coastal plain counties\nnext weekend. This will be worth monitoring for any freeze\nheadlines, as the end of the growing season is Nov 25 for our\nland forecast zones.",
